382
00:21:56,400 --> 00:22:00,240
And then the second question is how has it changed?

383
00:22:00,240 --> 00:22:04,960
And that almost always gives them an opportunity to talk about what's bad about their town

384
00:22:04,960 --> 00:22:11,280
without me delving into it, without me bringing it right to the surface.

385
00:22:11,280 --> 00:22:12,680
I let them do it.

386
00:22:12,680 --> 00:22:19,240
So what is the best thing about Concord?

387
00:22:19,240 --> 00:22:26,720
It's actually, we're so lucky that there are so many different good answers to that question.

388
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:33,480
And I think I'll try and answer it in light of some of the other places I've been to.

389
00:22:33,480 --> 00:22:36,000
First of all, it's a healthy town.

390
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:39,680
It has three downtowns really.

391
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:46,160
If you see downtown Concord, the Depot, and West Concord, those are three vibrant centers.

392
00:22:46,160 --> 00:22:47,160
Now there's challenges.

393
00:22:47,160 --> 00:22:54,480
There are stores that are open in the downtown and in the Depot area.

394
00:22:54,480 --> 00:23:04,280
But most small towns of 17,000 people, their downtown is gone.

395
00:23:04,280 --> 00:23:07,160
There's nothing there anymore.

396
00:23:07,160 --> 00:23:10,440
There's a pawn shop.

397
00:23:10,440 --> 00:23:13,680
There's an ATM.

398
00:23:13,680 --> 00:23:18,480
And just rows and rows of boarded up storefronts.

399
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:21,120
It's really quite sad.

400
00:23:21,120 --> 00:23:25,400
And outside the town there's a Dollar General or two.

401
00:23:25,400 --> 00:23:27,600
And that's it.

402
00:23:27,600 --> 00:23:33,320
So that's one thing I love about this town is it has a really vibrant downtown.

403
00:23:33,320 --> 00:23:41,360
It has such an interesting history, of course, going back to 1635.

404
00:23:41,360 --> 00:23:47,520
And people appreciate that history.

405
00:23:47,520 --> 00:23:54,740
And they also, one of the things that I really love about this town is the vigor with which

406
00:23:54,740 --> 00:23:57,640
we defend open spaces.

407
00:23:57,640 --> 00:24:00,360
There are so many towns in the greater Boston area.

408
00:24:00,360 --> 00:24:02,360
I grew up in Wilesom.

409
00:24:02,360 --> 00:24:06,840
There's no open spaces left in Wilesom, except for Wilesom College and along the Aqueduct

410
00:24:06,840 --> 00:24:07,840
in Wilesom.

411
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:15,080
They've developed everything because it raises their tax base and tax revenue or whatever.

412
00:24:15,080 --> 00:24:18,920
Here we've maintained, we value all this open space.

413
00:24:18,920 --> 00:24:25,320
So much of it has been donated and it's preserved either by the trustees or by the town or,

414
00:24:25,320 --> 00:24:29,160
of course, the National Park.

415
00:24:29,160 --> 00:24:33,440
So I love that part about the town.

416
00:24:33,440 --> 00:24:42,880
I think that also engenders a certain spirit in the town of volunteerism and so many passionate

417
00:24:42,880 --> 00:24:47,840
people working on different projects in the town.

418
00:24:47,840 --> 00:24:53,280
I've been absolutely struck by that since I volunteered in the winter for working with

419
00:24:53,280 --> 00:24:55,120
the 250th.

420
00:24:55,120 --> 00:25:03,640
How I was a little, my reasons to do it far outweighed my hesitations.

421
00:25:03,640 --> 00:25:07,080
My hesitations were, is this going to be parks and rec and we're not going to actually

422
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:08,080
do anything?

423
00:25:08,080 --> 00:25:16,720
But then I met the people who are involved with this already and one, they said, oh,

424
00:25:16,720 --> 00:25:18,520
you've been here for four or five years.

425
00:25:18,520 --> 00:25:19,520
Welcome.

426
00:25:19,520 --> 00:25:20,520
Welcome home.

427
00:25:20,520 --> 00:25:21,520
And I just felt welcome.

428
00:25:21,520 --> 00:25:23,760
I mean, that's a big deal to me.

429
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:28,400
And there are people involved with this that have been here for many generations.

430
00:25:28,400 --> 00:25:36,120
And I think that that struck me as it's a town that has a very rich history and the

431
00:25:36,120 --> 00:25:40,360
people I've met have been very welcoming of newcomers, at least so far, I'm sure.

432
00:25:40,360 --> 00:25:44,440
I'm sure as I go, I'll run across some others, but it's been really incredible.

433
00:25:44,440 --> 00:25:48,120
And I think this town is among the most engaging that I've ever lived in.

434
00:25:48,120 --> 00:25:52,800
It's been really wonderful.

435
00:25:52,800 --> 00:25:56,840
So the second question I ask is how has it changed?

436
00:25:56,840 --> 00:26:03,600
And there hasn't been that much change in the town overall.

437
00:26:03,600 --> 00:26:08,880
I think it has been a little bit yuppie-fied.

438
00:26:08,880 --> 00:26:14,600
There's definitely a hollowing out of middle class in Concord.

439
00:26:14,600 --> 00:26:21,440
You really, it's not so much of an age thing as a wealth matter.

440
00:26:21,440 --> 00:26:27,600
For a while there, it seemed to be like not too many young families were moving into town.

441
00:26:27,600 --> 00:26:32,400
Now in the last five years, I seem to feel like there's a new generation coming into

442
00:26:32,400 --> 00:26:40,240
the town with younger people, at least in my neighborhood.

443
00:26:40,240 --> 00:26:45,320
I'm now the old guy in the neighborhood.

444
00:26:45,320 --> 00:26:53,080
So that's, now the one problem with that is I don't think this younger generation has

445
00:26:53,080 --> 00:26:58,720
the same dedication to volunteerism that I've seen in other, the previous generation.

446
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:03,720
For instance, like they have to, I believe they have to hire in soccer coaches now.

447
00:27:03,720 --> 00:27:08,240
Where in the past it was just dad would volunteer, mom would volunteer.

448
00:27:08,240 --> 00:27:14,440
And there's a lot less of that apparent participation in the kids' lives.

449
00:27:14,440 --> 00:27:21,880
Not to say there isn't some of that, but I feel like there isn't quite the same level.

450
00:27:21,880 --> 00:27:24,200
I could see that as a teacher.

451
00:27:24,200 --> 00:27:34,800
Events that used to be run by parents just trying to get people to do some work on some

452
00:27:34,800 --> 00:27:37,000
of that stuff was harder.

453
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:38,000
Interesting.

454
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:42,560
Not that it didn't happen, it just became more difficult and maybe less rich in terms

455
00:27:42,560 --> 00:27:46,360
of the participation.

456
00:27:46,360 --> 00:27:52,120
The one obvious glaring drawback in town is a lack of racial diversity.

457
00:27:52,120 --> 00:28:00,120
It's something that I've been involved with since the, really since I moved into town

458
00:28:00,120 --> 00:28:07,520
trying to see what can be done.

459
00:28:07,520 --> 00:28:09,640
When I got my job at Fenton, I said, oh, this is great.

460
00:28:09,640 --> 00:28:13,760
I'm so glad to get my job, but you really should have hired a black guy or a black woman

461
00:28:13,760 --> 00:28:16,520
to do this because you need it at this school.

462
00:28:16,520 --> 00:28:17,520
You don't have diversity.

463
00:28:17,520 --> 00:28:21,600
Well, when I left, the same questions were being asked.

464
00:28:21,600 --> 00:28:28,880
For twenty-three, twenty-four years, it's been a struggle and a very active struggle.

465
00:28:28,880 --> 00:28:33,520
It's not like we've been really trying to find solutions to this and trying to get this

466
00:28:33,520 --> 00:28:34,520
town a more welcoming town.

467
00:28:34,520 --> 00:28:41,080
That's one of the reasons I got very much involved with Robin's House and then Friends

468
00:28:41,080 --> 00:28:42,080
of Ellen.

469
00:28:42,080 --> 00:28:43,080
It's interesting, guys.

470
00:28:43,080 --> 00:28:48,080
I never talk politics when I travel.

471
00:28:48,080 --> 00:28:49,080
I can't.

472
00:28:49,080 --> 00:28:55,400
I know that there's a lot of... I'm surrounded in a red state estate and I've got Massachusetts

473
00:28:55,400 --> 00:28:59,000
plates on my car.

474
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:09,040
I'm pretty used to not talking politics with strangers, but in my town, it's harder to

475
00:29:09,040 --> 00:29:15,440
not talk a little bit about the important values of this town.

476
00:29:15,440 --> 00:29:23,040
I think in terms of at least the ideals that the groups here have been effective, I would

477
00:29:23,040 --> 00:29:29,640
say from my perspective of not effective in changing demographics here yet, but in terms

478
00:29:29,640 --> 00:29:35,800
of raising awareness and importance of it, I've talked to several high school seniors

479
00:29:35,800 --> 00:29:43,060
who I do interviews for my alma mater when they're interviewing for admission.

480
00:29:43,060 --> 00:29:47,760
In the course of every conversation I've had with a Concord student who's applying, that

481
00:29:47,760 --> 00:29:50,520
issue has come up as something very important to them in their lives.

482
00:29:50,520 --> 00:29:55,160
I think that the work that the town has done the last few years has been... It's a slow

483
00:29:55,160 --> 00:29:56,160
climb.

484
00:29:56,160 --> 00:30:02,760
It's a slow boulder to move up that mountain, but I do believe that the town has been effective

485
00:30:02,760 --> 00:30:11,800
in at least bubbling up the importance of these ideas and how to actually make a population

486
00:30:11,800 --> 00:30:12,800
shift happen.

487
00:30:12,800 --> 00:30:16,520
I don't think anybody knows how to actually do that.

